---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LegalDoc from '../components/LegalDoc.astro';
import { UMAMI, NEWSLETTER, CONTACT_EMAIL, url, WATCHDOG } from '../config';
---
<Base title="Privacy" description="How NeonKey handles your data: what is collected, cookieless Umami analytics, and your choices.">
  <LegalDoc eyebrow="Privacy" title="Privacy policy" updated={{ iso: '2026-11-13', text: 'Last updated November 13, 2026.' }} sections={[{ id: 'analytics', label: 'Analytics' }, { id: 'advertising', label: 'Advertising' }, { id: 'newsletter', label: 'Newsletter' }, { id: 'contact', label: 'Contact' }, { id: 'payments', label: 'Payments' }, { id: 'email-lockdown', label: 'Email Authentication Setup' }, { id: 'follow-up-desk', label: 'Follow-Up Desk' }, { id: 'client-files-and-access', label: 'Client files and access' }, { id: 'ai-inquiry-triage-demo-lab', label: 'AI inquiry triage demo (Lab)' }, { id: 'other-ai-demos-lab', label: 'Other AI demos (Lab)' }, { id: 'service-quiz', label: 'Service quiz' }, { id: 'free-it-kit', label: 'Free IT kit' }, ...(WATCHDOG !== 'off' ? [{ id: 'founder-watchdog', label: 'Founder Watchdog' }] : []), { id: 'founder-watchdog-waitlist', label: 'Founder Watchdog waitlist (closed)' }, { id: 'scam-check', label: 'Family Shield free check' }, { id: 'waitlists', label: 'Waitlists' }, { id: 'cookies', label: 'Cookies' }, { id: 'hosting', label: 'Hosting' }, { id: 'your-choices', label: 'Your choices' }]}>

    <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
    {UMAMI.websiteId ? (
      <p>NeonKey uses <a href="https://umami.is" data-umami-event="outbound-click" data-umami-event-item="umami-site">Umami</a> for privacy-friendly analytics. Umami doesn't use cookies, doesn't collect personal data and doesn't track you across sites. It records anonymous page views and a few button clicks (for example newsletter signups and service enquiries) so I can see what's useful. Blocking the script won't break anything on the site.</p>
    ) : (
      <p>This build doesn't load any analytics. If analytics are turned on, NeonKey will use Umami, a cookieless, privacy-friendly tool that records anonymous page views and button clicks only.</p>
    )}

    <h2 id="advertising">Advertising</h2>
    <p>NeonKey runs ads on Google and uses Google Ads conversion tracking (the Google tag) to see whether those ads lead to visits and enquiries. The tag is loaded from Google on every page and may set cookies or collect device and interaction data, such as the pages you view and whether you send a message through the contact form, to measure ad performance. See <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">how Google uses information from sites or apps that use its services</a>. Blocking the script won't break anything on the site.</p>
    <p>The same Google tag also runs Google Analytics, which measures visits and where they come from (for example search, ads or other sites) and may likewise use cookies and collect device and interaction data. The <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">same Google page</a> explains how Google uses this information.</p>
    <p>On the newsletter signup page and its thank-you page only, NeonKey also loads the Meta (Facebook and Instagram) pixel and the Reddit pixel. They tell Meta and Reddit when someone who saw a NeonKey ad visits the signup page or subscribes, so those ads can be measured. They may set cookies and collect device and interaction data. They don't receive your email address. See <a href="https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/">Meta's privacy policy</a> and <a href="https://www.reddit.com/policies/privacy-policy">Reddit's privacy policy</a>. Blocking them won't break anything on the site.</p>

    <h2 id="newsletter">Newsletter</h2>
    {NEWSLETTER.mode !== 'off' ? (
      <>
        <p>Unlock the Week is sent with <a href="https://kit.com/privacy" data-umami-event="outbound-click" data-umami-event-item="kit-privacy">Kit</a> (formerly ConvertKit), which processes subscriber data on NeonKey's behalf. When you subscribe, your email address goes straight to Kit. Kit stores it along with basic sign-up and delivery data, such as when and where you signed up and whether an issue was delivered, opened or clicked.</p>
        <p>Kit sends a confirmation email first, and you're only added once you click it. If you haven't confirmed after about a day, I send you one reminder through <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>, NeonKey's email delivery provider, with a button to get a fresh confirmation link. That's the only reminder: if you don't confirm, you won't hear from me again, and the reminder itself has an unsubscribe link.</p>
        <p>Your email is used only to send the newsletter and is never sold. Every issue has an unsubscribe link, and you can ask for your data to be deleted at any time by emailing {CONTACT_EMAIL}.</p>
        {NEWSLETTER.mode === 'embed' && (
          <p>The signup form on this site is loaded from Kit. Kit's form script may store a small cookie or local-storage entry, for example to remember that you've already subscribed.</p>
        )}
      </>
    ) : (
      <p>Newsletter signups aren't open yet, so no email addresses are collected. When they open, Unlock the Week will be sent with Kit (formerly ConvertKit), and this section will describe what Kit stores.</p>
    )}

    <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
    <p>The contact form is delivered by <a href="https://web3forms.com/privacy">Web3Forms</a>, which passes your name, email, topic and message to {CONTACT_EMAIL}. Messages, whether sent through the form or by email, are kept only as long as needed to reply and are never sold.</p>

    <h2 id="payments">Payments</h2>
    <p>Online payments, such as the Email Authentication Setup checkout, the Monthly Remote Support Plan and invoices, are handled by <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy">Stripe</a>. Stripe collects your card details directly, and NeonKey never sees or stores your full card number. Stripe shares your name, email, business name (if you give one), what you bought and the amount with NeonKey so I can deliver the work, send receipts and handle refunds. Stripe keeps payment records as its own policy and the law require.</p>

    <h2 id="email-lockdown">Email Authentication Setup</h2>
    <p>When you buy the <a href={url('/services/email-lockdown/')}>Email Authentication Setup</a>, Stripe passes the domain you type at checkout, along with your name, email and business name, to a small NeonKey service on Cloudflare Workers. It checks your domain's public DNS records (the same records anyone can look up) and emails your report through <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>. The report is turned into a PDF with Cloudflare's browser rendering service, which processes the report itself, not any other data. No passwords are needed or collected.</p>
    <p>Your order record (name, email, business name, domain and check results) is kept in Cloudflare for 180 days, so I can run your rechecks, answer questions and deal with any payment dispute. After that it deletes automatically. Copies of the emails sent to you, and order notices sent to NeonKey at {CONTACT_EMAIL}, are kept in NeonKey's business email as business records. To have them deleted sooner, email {CONTACT_EMAIL} (payment records Stripe must keep are the exception).</p>

    <h2 id="follow-up-desk">Follow-Up Desk</h2>
    <p>When you sign up for the <a href={url('/follow-up-desk/')}>Follow-Up Desk</a>, Stripe passes your name, email and business name to a small NeonKey service on Cloudflare Workers, which creates your account. What you enter on your dashboard (your business details, your message wording, and each customer's name, email, job and date) is stored there so the follow-ups can go out. Follow-ups, replies and your reports are sent through <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>. When a customer replies, their message and any attachments are passed straight to your inbox and are not kept by the service. No passwords are used: your dashboard link is your login, and only a scrambled fingerprint of it is stored.</p>
    <p>NeonKey uses this data only to run the service for you. It is never sold, never used for NeonKey's own marketing, and the follow-ups and replies are not copied to NeonKey's inbox. Closed follow-ups are removed 90 days after they close. A backup copy is made each night on Cloudflare and kept for 7 days, so the service can be restored if something breaks. When your subscription ends, sending stops right away and everything is deleted 30 days later (in case you come back), and it leaves the backups within 7 days after that. Sign-up and billing notices sent to NeonKey at {CONTACT_EMAIL} are kept as business records. To have your data deleted sooner, email {CONTACT_EMAIL}. You're responsible for only adding people who asked you for an estimate or are already your customers; anyone who gets a follow-up can stop them with one click.</p>

    <h2 id="client-files-and-access">Client files and access</h2>
    <p>Some work needs files or access from you, such as a spreadsheet export, your FAQ or price list, or a helper invite to your domain or admin settings. I ask only for what the job needs, and I never ask for your passwords, card or bank details, or other sensitive personal information. If you send something like that by mistake, I delete it and let you know.</p>
    <p>Files you send for a free report are deleted within 7 days after the report is sent. Files for paid work are deleted within 30 days after handoff, unless you ask me to keep them. Deleting means removing them from NeonKey's email (including the trash), any copies and any saved files. Client files are never shared or sold, and they're never pasted into the free Lab demos. At handoff, you remove my access (or I confirm it's removed), and every admin login stays with you.</p>

    <h2 id="ai-inquiry-triage-demo-lab">AI inquiry triage demo (Lab)</h2>
    <p>The <a href={url('/lab/ai-inquiry-triage/')}>AI inquiry triage demo</a> sends the text you type to a small NeonKey API running on Cloudflare Workers. The API forwards it to Google's <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms">Gemini API</a> to generate the result, then returns the result to your browser. NeonKey doesn't store or log the message or the result.</p>
    <p>The demo uses Gemini's free tier. Under Google's terms, content sent on the free tier may be used by Google to improve its products, and human reviewers may read it. <strong>Please don't paste private, sensitive or personal information into the demo.</strong> Because of those terms, the demo isn't available in the UK, Switzerland or the EEA.</p>
    <p>To enforce rate limits (5 runs a minute and 30 a day per visitor, plus a daily total), the API keeps short-lived counters in Cloudflare: a one-way hash of your IP address with the date, and a run count. The counters expire after 48 hours. Cloudflare, as the host, may also process standard request data such as your IP address; see <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="other-ai-demos-lab">Other AI demos (Lab)</h2>
    <p>Three more Lab demos work the same way as the triage demo: what you send goes through a small NeonKey API on Cloudflare Workers to Google's <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/terms">Gemini API</a> on the free tier, the result comes back to your browser, and NeonKey doesn't store or log what you send or the result. The same free-tier warning applies: <strong>please don't send private, sensitive or personal information</strong>, and these demos aren't available in the UK, Switzerland or the EEA.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <a href={url('/lab/review-reply/')}>review reply demo</a> sends the review text you type. It shares the triage demo's API and limits (5 runs a minute and 30 a day per visitor).</li>
      <li>The <a href={url('/lab/office-ai/')}>Office AI demo</a> sends the question you type (up to 400 characters); the documents it answers from are made-up samples. Limits: 5 runs a minute and 30 a day per visitor, plus a daily total.</li>
      <li>The <a href={url('/lab/receipt-sorter/')}>receipt sorter demo</a> sends the receipt photo or PDF you choose (up to 5 MB). Limits: 5 runs a minute and 20 a day per visitor, plus a daily total.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each API keeps the same kind of short-lived rate-limit counters as the triage demo: a one-way hash of your IP address with the date and a run count, deleted 48 hours after last use.</p>

    <h2 id="service-quiz">Service quiz</h2>
    <p>The <a href={url('/services/quiz/')}>"What service do I need?" quiz</a> works out your result in your browser. To see it, you enter your email address, and your full plan is emailed to you as well.</p>
    <p>When you do, your email address, optional first name and quiz answers are sent to NeonKey through a small API on Cloudflare Workers to prepare your plan. The plan is emailed to you once through <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>, NeonKey's email delivery provider, and a copy with your email and answers is sent to NeonKey at {CONTACT_EMAIL} so I can prepare and follow up if you reply. That copy is kept only as long as needed and is never sold. You're only added to the newsletter if you tick the newsletter box; then Kit sends you a confirmation email first (see Newsletter above).</p>
    <p>To limit abuse, the API keeps short-lived counters in Cloudflare: a one-way hash of your IP address with the date, a one-way hash of your email address (so the same address can't be sent more than one plan a day), and a daily total. These expire after about 24 hours, and your answers aren't stored there. To have your quiz data deleted, email {CONTACT_EMAIL}.</p>

    <h2 id="free-it-kit">Free IT kit</h2>
    <p>If you ask for the <a href={url('/free-it-kit/')}>free Starter IT Kit</a>, your email address and anything optional you add (first name, business name, team size and biggest tech headache) are sent to NeonKey through the same small API on Cloudflare Workers as the service quiz. The download link is emailed to you once through <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>, and a copy with those details is sent to NeonKey at {CONTACT_EMAIL} so we can follow up about the kit. That copy is kept only as long as needed and is never sold. You're only added to the newsletter if you tick the newsletter box; then Kit sends you a confirmation email first. The same short-lived, hashed abuse counters described under Service quiz apply, kept separately for the kit. To have this data deleted, email {CONTACT_EMAIL}.</p>
    {WATCHDOG !== 'off' && <Fragment>
    <h2 id="founder-watchdog">Founder Watchdog</h2>
    <p>When you subscribe to <a href={url('/founders/watchdog/')}>Founder Watchdog</a>, Stripe handles the payment (see Payments above) and passes NeonKey your email address, the domain you typed at checkout, your plan and your subscription status. Those details, plus the results of each check on that domain (its public DNS records, whether your homepage loads, the registration expiry date and which alerts we've sent), are stored in Cloudflare (Workers KV) so the daily checks can run and spot changes. The checks only read public information about the domain; nothing about your visitors or your mailbox is collected. Watchdog emails (the setup step, alerts and the monthly all-clear) are sent through <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>, and NeonKey gets a short note at {CONTACT_EMAIL} when someone subscribes or can't finish setup. When your subscription ends, we stop checking and the stored record deletes itself after 30 days. It's never sold or shared. To have it deleted sooner, email {CONTACT_EMAIL}.</p>
    </Fragment>}
    <h2 id="founder-watchdog-waitlist">Founder Watchdog waitlist (closed)</h2>
    <p>For a short time in November 2026, Founder Watchdog had a waitlist instead of a checkout. If you joined it, your email address, the domain you named and your answers (who manages its DNS, how many domains you run, what you'd want watched and whether you'd pay the planned price), plus which NeonKey page you came from, were sent to NeonKey through the same small API on Cloudflare Workers as the service quiz. That entry is kept for up to one year so we can email you once about the launch, then it deletes automatically. Joining didn't start any monitoring of your domain, and it's never sold or shared. To have your entry deleted sooner, email {CONTACT_EMAIL}.</p>
    <h2 id="scam-check">Family Shield free check</h2>
    <p>The free check on the <a href={url('/scam-checker/')}>scam checker</a> and <a href={url('/learn/is-this-a-scam/')}>Is this message a scam?</a> pages runs entirely in your browser. The message you paste is never sent to NeonKey or anyone else, and it isn't saved. We only count that a check ran, with the result and which warning signs showed up (for example "urgent" or "look-alike link"), through the anonymous analytics described above, never the text itself. Before Nov 13, 2026 this page had a Family Shield waitlist; anyone who joined it is kept only to send the one email we promised, then deleted.</p>

    <h2 id="waitlists">Waitlists</h2>
    <p>Some pages may let you join a waitlist for something NeonKey is thinking about building. If you join, your email address, the optional answer you pick (for example, who you'd use it for) and the page you arrived from are sent through the same small API on Cloudflare Workers as the service quiz and saved there. We send you one confirmation through <a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy">Resend</a>, a copy comes to NeonKey at {CONTACT_EMAIL}, and we email you once when the product opens or if we decide not to build it. You aren't added to the newsletter, nothing is charged, and joining doesn't commit you to anything. To be removed, reply "remove" to the confirmation or email {CONTACT_EMAIL}. When a waitlist test ends, its list is deleted.</p>
    <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
    <p>NeonKey itself sets no cookies, but the Google tag and, on the newsletter pages, the Meta and Reddit pixels (see Advertising above) may set cookies to measure ad performance. Links to other sites, such as retailers, lead to pages that may set their own cookies.</p>

    <h2 id="hosting">Hosting</h2>
    <p>The site is hosted on <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub Pages</a>. Like any web host, GitHub may process standard request data such as your IP address to serve and secure the site.</p>

    <h2 id="your-choices">Your choices</h2>
    <p>To ask what data NeonKey holds about you, or to have it deleted, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
  </LegalDoc>
</Base>
